          <p class="text-sm">Lecture 1 asked: is this draft editable? Lecture 2 asks: will a listener stay for 20 seconds — chorus, conflict, hook.</p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Guest: Fran, an award winner in a China AI music creation contest, will speak briefly on what the process felt like and what she would tell someone still revising a first batch of songs.
        </p>

        <!-- Five Lectures Curriculum -->
        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group text-purple-400"></i>
            <span>Season 1 Curriculum</span>
          </h3>
          <ol class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-300 pl-2">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-slate-500 font-semibold">1</span>
              <span><strong>Sep 12</strong>: From Idea to an Editable Track: Structure, Lyrics, Style Tokens, Negative Prompts <span class="text-slate-500 text-xs">(Ended)</span></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-purple-300 font-semibold">
              <span class="font-mono text-purple-400">2</span>
              <span><strong>Sep 26</strong>: Structure and Emotion: Chorus, Conflict, and Holding 20 Seconds <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-300 text-xs border border-purple-500/30">This Lecture</span></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-purple-400 font-semibold">3</span>
              <span><strong>Oct 10</strong>: Chinese Vocals and Traditional Instrument Timbres</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-purple-400 font-semibold">4</span>
              <span><strong>Oct 24</strong>: Arrangement Layers and Cinematic Imagery</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-purple-400 font-semibold">5</span>
              <span><strong>Nov 7</strong>: Final Delivery: Version Selection and Documenting Revisions</span>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- Clock Schedule -->
        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-clock text-purple-400"></i>
            <span>Clock Schedule</span>
          </h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-300 pl-2">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-purple-400 font-semibold min-w-[75px]">5:30–5:50</span>
              <span>Chorus function, conflict curve, 20-second hook. Compare two cuts. Pass/fail on “does it hold,” not taste.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-amber-400 font-semibold min-w-[75px]">5:50–6:00</span>
              <span><strong>Guest Fran</strong>: contest experience, what she noticed later about structure and emotion, practical advice (8–10 minutes)</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-slate-500 font-semibold min-w-[75px]">6:00–6:05</span>
              <span class="text-slate-400">Break (5 minutes)</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-purple-400 font-semibold min-w-[75px]">6:05–6:25</span>
              <span>Agents help rewrite structure; they do not ghostwrite the song. Brief on Jev (TypeSafe AI, 15 Sep 2026): a System One decision model. It returns typed scores, not lyrics. Generation stays with an LLM / Suno-class tool; judging a hook can be a Jev-class call.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
              <span class="font-mono text-purple-400 font-semibold min-w-[75px]">6:25–6:30</span>
              <span>Audience Q&A</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Takeaways -->
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-800 space-y-3 text-sm">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-gift text-purple-400"></i>
            <span>What You Will Take Away</span>
          </h4>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed list-disc list-inside">
            <li><strong>A 20-second checklist</strong>: entry, persona/setting, conflict, chorus promise</li>
            <li><strong>Three reusable prompts</strong>: for structure rewriting rather than asking for more adjectives</li>
            <li><strong>Jev's role in the creative loop</strong>: LLM generates candidates, decision model scores. It cannot hallucinate out-of-schema text, but human judgment verifies the final cut</li>
            <li><strong>Fran's field insights</strong>: which section to rework, what to listen for, and avoiding the trap of one-shot perfection</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Guest Speaker Section -->
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-950/20 via-slate-900 to-slate-900 border border-amber-500/30 space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-user-check text-amber-400"></i>
            <span>Guest Speaker: Fran</span>
          </h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Award-winning creator in a domestic AI music creation competition. In this lecture, Fran will share three things: her authentic experience going through the competition, what she later realized about structure and emotion, and practical advice for creators revising their first batch of tracks. No contest entry prerequisites, no scoring rubrics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Jev Explain Section -->
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-indigo-950/20 border border-indigo-500/30 space-y-3 text-sm">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-bolt text-indigo-400"></i>
            <span>Jev in One Paragraph (For Non-Technical Audiences)</span>
          </h4>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Released by TypeSafe AI on Sep 15, 2026 (current public release approx. jev-1.13). Jev is not a chatbot. You send state plus typed questions; it returns a choice, a score, or a yes/no with confidence, in roughly 70–500 ms. It is billed per input. It fits narrow assessments like "does this chorus hold a hook?" rather than "write this song for me."
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-400 text-xs border-t border-indigo-900/50 pt-2">
            This session covers its practical boundaries without sales pitches or registration requirements.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Difference from Lecture 1 -->
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 space-y-2 text-sm">
          <p class="font-semibold text-white">Differences from Lecture 1</p>
          <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-slate-300">
            <li>• <strong>Lecture 1</strong>: What makes a draft editable, and general RSI (bounded self-improvement exists; unconstrained models designing next-gen models has not occurred).</li>
            <li>• <strong>Lecture 2</strong>: Audible standards for structure and emotion, decoupling generation models from decision models, and a guest reflection from an award-winning creator.</li>
          </ul>
